<Project>

  <!--
    Repository-wide build settings. Applies to EVERY project — src, tests, examples, prototypes —
    so only things that are harmless everywhere belong here. Package metadata and documentation
    generation live in src/Directory.Build.props instead: GenerateDocumentationFile in particular
    turns on CS1591 (missing XML comment) for every public member, which is right for the shipped
    libraries and pure noise in a test project.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Deterministic builds are already the SDK default; stated explicitly because
      ContinuousIntegrationBuild below is only meaningful alongside it, and a future edit that
      turned it off would silently defeat that.
    -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Dependency version policy, stated once for the whole repository. Only the numbers live
      here — nothing is referenced from this file, so a project that does not want a package
      simply does not mention it.

      WHY THE SHIPPED PACKAGES BRACKET BECKHOFF AND NOT THESE. Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads.csproj pins
      Beckhoff to [7.0.292,8.0.0) and explains why at length: a major version could reshape the
      value-decode contract while preserving every signature. The reasoning below is not a
      weaker version of that argument, it is a different one, and the difference is not
      "Microsoft is better behaved across majors". A ceiling only works on a package that
      nothing ELSE in the consumer's graph references. Beckhoff.TwinCAT.Ads is a leaf — this
      repository is the only thing asking for it — so its ceiling costs a consumer nothing worse
      than a restore error a maintainer can evaluate deliberately. ASP.NET Core references
      Microsoft.Extensions.* directly, so [8.0.0,9.0.0) here would be an unresolvable conflict
      for any net9 or net10 web app that referenced this package, unfixable from the consumer's
      end short of dropping the package. A ceiling there breaks working consumers rather than
      protecting them.

      FIXED, NOT FLOATING. "8.*" packs as ">= whatever nuget.org happened to hold on the day the
      release workflow ran", which makes the declared floor of a shipped package a function of
      build timing — the opposite of what Deterministic and ContinuousIntegrationBuild above are
      here to buy. These are the lowest versions carrying the APIs this repository uses; a
      consumer whose own graph wants newer lifts them, which is exactly what the absent ceiling
      is for.

      PER TARGET FRAMEWORK, because the shipped packages multi-target net8/net9/net10 and a
      single 8.x line for all three hands a net10 consumer 8.x abstractions unless something
      else in their graph lifts them. For a cross-targeting project $(TargetFramework) is a
      global property during each per-framework inner build, so it is already set when this file
      is evaluated and each leg gets its own major — verified in the packed nuspec, which has a
      dependency group per framework.

      net8.0 is the UNCONDITIONED default rather than a fourth condition, and that matters: a
      project with a single <TargetFramework> sets it in its own body, which is AFTER this file
      is imported, so the conditions below would all miss and the version would come out empty
      (NU1015). Every single-target project in this repository is net8.0, so the default is
      right for all of them; one added later on a newer framework would silently take the 8.0.0
      floor, which is safe but stale. Give it <TargetFrameworks> (plural, one value) if that
      ever matters — the cross-targeting build sets the property early enough.

      Tests, examples and prototypes use the same properties. They ship nothing, so their floors
      matter less — but a test project pinned to a line BELOW what the library it references
      declares is a package downgrade (NU1605, an error here), which is how a version bump in
      src would otherwise break the build somewhere that looks unrelated to it.
    -->
    <MicrosoftExtensionsVersion>8.0.0</MicrosoftExtensionsVersion>
    <MicrosoftExtensionsVersion Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net9.0'">9.0.0</MicrosoftExtensionsVersion>
    <MicrosoftExtensionsVersion Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'">10.0.0</MicrosoftExtensionsVersion>

    <!--
      System.Reactive ships one line rather than one per .NET major, so there is no target
      framework to match it to. Fixed and unbounded for the same reasons as above, and the
      shared-graph argument applies to it harder than to anything else here: a consumer of an Rx
      companion package references System.Reactive themselves nearly by definition.

      6.1.0 rather than 6.0.0, which is not an arbitrary choice of patch. Beckhoff.TwinCAT.Ads
      7.0.292 itself depends on System.Reactive >= 6.1.0, so anything lower is a downgrade the
      moment the Alarms package is restored beside the core one. A floor is the lowest version
      that actually RESOLVES, not the lowest that compiles.
    -->
    <SystemReactiveVersion>6.1.0</SystemReactiveVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">
    <!--
      Normalises the absolute source paths baked into the PDB to deterministic placeholders, so a
      package built on a hosted runner is bit-for-bit reproducible and does not carry
      /home/runner/work/... around inside it. Those normalised paths are exactly what SourceLink
      maps back to github.com URLs, so this and SourceLink only pay off together.

      Keyed off GITHUB_ACTIONS rather than passed as -p: on each workflow step, so a new workflow
      (or a new pack step in an existing one) cannot forget it. A LOCAL build deliberately does not
      set it: normalised paths would stop the debugger finding the sources sitting on disk.
    -->
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
